      <!-- Sub-part C: Standard Extensions Deep-Dives -->
      <div class="isa-subtopic-block" style="margin-bottom: 1.75rem;">
        <h4 class="isa-subtopic-title">
          <span class="pill-badge badge-teal">MODULAR EXTENSIONS</span>
          Standard Extensions Deep-Dive: Spending Silicon to Conquer the Memory Wall
        </h4>
        <p class="isa-subtopic-text">
          In application processors, architectural extensions represent direct silicon gate investments that eliminate CPU stalls, reduce memory bandwidth pressure, and accelerate critical OS kernel algorithms:
        </p>

        <div class="isa-extensions-flow">
          <!-- Extension RV64I -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-cyan">BASE RV64I</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">64-Bit Base Integer Architecture (Flat Virtual Addressing)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: ~45 kGE Baseline (Core ALUs &amp; 64-bit RF)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Implements 32 64-bit general-purpose registers, 64-bit adders, shifters, and memory address generation units (AGU). Provides dedicated <code>*W</code> instructions (<code>addw</code>, <code>subw</code>, <code>sllw</code>, <code>addiw</code>) that perform 32-bit operations and automatically sign-extend results to bit 63.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> Provides a flat virtual address space spanning up to 256 Terabytes (Sv48), eliminating 32-bit physical RAM boundaries and supporting multi-gigabyte memory-mapped files without banking tricks.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Mandatory baseline for all evaluated application cores (Rocket, CVA6, BlackParrot, BOOM).
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extension M -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-yellow">EXTENSION 'M'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Hardware 64-Bit Multiply &amp; Divide (Radix-4 Booth &amp; SRT)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +8 to 18 kGE (Multiplier Array &amp; Iterative Divider)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Pipelined Radix-4 Booth multiplier tree calculating full 64&times;64 products in 2 to 3 clock cycles, alongside an iterative restoring or non-restoring SRT divider computing quotients over 32 to 64 clock cycles.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> <strong>25&times; to 60&times; acceleration</strong> over GCC software emulation routines (<code>__muldi3</code>, <code>__divdi3</code>). Eliminates severe thread scheduling stalls during kernel hash table lookups, timer tick calculations, and slab allocator maths.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Universal in all evaluated application processors. Rocket integrates a non-blocking iterative divider; BOOM features fully pipelined multi-cycle multiplier stages.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extension A -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-teal">EXTENSION 'A'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Atomic Memory Operations (AMO &amp; LR/SC Cache Locking)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +3 to 6 kGE + L1 Reservation Monitor</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Load-Reserved/Store-Conditional (<code>lr.d</code>/<code>sc.d</code>) and single-cycle Atomic Read-Modify-Write (<code>amoadd.d</code>, <code>amoswap.d</code>, <code>amomax.d</code>). The L1 Data Cache incorporates an address reservation latch that monitors bus invalidation snoops.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> <strong>The foundation of Linux SMP multi-core scaling.</strong> Powers kernel spinlocks, mutexes, futexes, reader-writer semaphores, RCU (Read-Copy-Update), and atomic reference counting without system deadlocks.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Mandatory for Linux multi-core boot. CVA6 and BlackParrot implement AMO ALU logic directly inside the L1 cache controller to prevent bus lock contention.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extensions F & D -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-coral">EXTENSIONS 'F' &amp; 'D'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">IEEE 754-2008 Floating-Point Units (Hard-Float Datapath)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +35 to 65 kGE (FMA Multiplier, Align &amp; Normalizer)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> 32 64-bit FPRs (<code>f0&ndash;f31</code>) and a multi-stage pipelined Fused Multiply-Add (FMA) unit executing <code>(rs1 &times; rs2) + rs3</code> with single intermediate rounding. Supports IEEE 754 subnormals, infinities, quiet/signaling NaNs, and NaN-boxing.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> Satisfies the <code>lp64d</code> Linux ABI, executing floating-point software (BLAS, Python NumPy, multimedia codecs, browser engines) at full native silicon speeds without triggering kernel soft-float trap handlers.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Rocket and CVA6 feature modular Berkeley HardFloat units; SonicBOOM integrates dual fully pipelined FP execution pipelines.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Extension C -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-lime">EXTENSION 'C'</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Compressed 16-Bit Encodings (Cache Bandwidth Multiplier)</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +1.5 to 3.5 kGE (Fetch Expander &amp; Alignment MUXes)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Re-encodes the most common 64-bit instructions into 16-bit equivalents (<code>c.ld</code>, <code>c.sd</code>, <code>c.addiw</code>, <code>c.addi</code>). The Instruction Fetch unit handles halfword-aligned instruction streams and expands them into 32-bit instructions before decode.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>The Memory Bandwidth Payoff:</strong> Shrinks compiled Linux kernel and userland binaries by <strong>25% to 35%</strong>. This increases effective L1-I cache hit rate by <strong>15% to 20%</strong>, directly reducing external memory bus traffic.
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> Essential for maximizing instruction fetch efficiency in modern application pipelines.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Privileged Architecture & MMU -->
          <div class="isa-ext-card">
            <div class="isa-ext-head">
              <div class="isa-ext-badge-group">
                <span class="isa-ext-badge badge-ink">PRIVILEGE &amp; MMU</span>
                <span class="isa-ext-name">Privileged Rings, PMP Firewalls &amp; Sv39/Sv48 Virtual Memory</span>
              </div>
              <div class="isa-cost-pill">Silicon Cost: +20 to 45 kGE (Hardware PTW, TLBs, CSR Bank)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="isa-ext-body">
              <p>
                <strong>The Mechanism:</strong> Machine (M), Supervisor (S), and User (U) privilege rings. Hardware Page Table Walker (PTW) autonomously traverses 3-level (Sv39) or 4-level (Sv48) page tables in memory on TLB misses, populating translation entries without software intervention.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Software Payoff:</strong> The physical foundation of modern OS security: enforces process address space isolation, demand paging, memory-mapped I/O protection, and copy-on-write (<code>fork</code>).
              </p>
              <div class="isa-adoption-note">
                <strong>Adoption Decision:</strong> The non-negotiable definition of an application-class processor.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

