            <h3 class="ladder-title">High-Performance Out-of-Order Superscalar Engine (SonicBOOM)</h3>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ladder-body">
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Problem:</strong> The Memory Wall (100+ clock cycles for DRAM accesses) and instruction dependency chains place an insurmountable barrier on in-order cores: sustained CPI rarely drops below 1.3 in real-world workloads, capping single-thread performance regardless of clock frequency.
          </p>
          <p class="ladder-narrative">
            <strong>The Architectural Solution:</strong> Transition to a multi-wide (2 to 4-wide) out-of-order superscalar pipeline. Map 32 architectural registers onto a 96-128 entry unified Physical Register File (PRF) to eliminate false WAR and WAW hazards. Dispatch micro-ops (uOPs) to distributed reservation stations, dynamically hide memory latency using a non-blocking L1-D cache with 8-entry MSHRs ("hits-under-miss"), and commit up to 4 instructions per cycle through a 128-entry circular Reorder Buffer (RoB).
          </p>
