    <!-- SUBSECTION 2.1: ARCHETYPE 1 DEEP-DIVE -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <div id="archetype-1-deepdive" class="concept-stream" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <div class="concept-step-card archetype-coral-border">
        <div class="step-card-header">
          <span class="step-tag badge-coral">2.1 ARCHITECTURAL DEEP-DIVE</span>
          <h3 class="step-card-title">Archetype 1: Classic In-Order Baseline (Rocket Chip)</h3>
        </div>
        <p class="step-card-lead">
          Designed by UC Berkeley and maintained under the Chips Alliance, <strong>Rocket Chip</strong> is the preeminent open-source reference implementation of the 64-bit RISC-V privileged architecture. It proves how an elegant, classical 5-stage pipeline can satisfy all operating system requirements (Sv39 virtual memory, double-precision IEEE-754 arithmetic, and machine/supervisor privilege rings) within a tiny silicon footprint of just ~135 kGE.
        </p>

        <!-- 2.1.1 Rocket Chip Technical Breakdown -->
        <div class="sub-topic-card">
          <div class="sub-topic-head">
            <div class="sub-topic-title-group">
              <span class="pill-badge badge-coral">CORE 1</span>
              <h4 class="sub-topic-title">Rocket Chip &bull; 5-Stage In-Order RISC Core</h4>
            </div>
            <span class="sub-topic-spec-badge">~135 kGE &bull; Chisel HDL</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            Rocket represents the purest distillation of Patterson's classical RISC pipeline principles scaled to 64-bit computing:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Classical 5-Stage Single-Issue Pipeline:</strong> Instructions progress through Instruction Fetch (IF), Instruction Decode (ID), Execute (EX), Memory Access (MEM), and Writeback (WB). Branch decisions and target addresses are resolved in the EX stage, incurring a modest 2-cycle penalty on mispredicted control transfers.</li>
            <li><strong>Decoupled TileLink-UH Interconnect:</strong> Rocket communicates with the memory system using TileLink-UH (Uncached Heavyweight). The bus protocol uses decoupled request (Channel A: Get, PutFullData, PutPartialData) and response (Channel D: AccessAck, AccessAckData) channels with ready/valid handshakes, natively supporting multi-beat cache line bursts without bus deadlocks.</li>
            <li><strong>Direct-Mapped or Set-Associative VIPT Caches:</strong> The L1 instruction and data caches use Virtual-Index Physical-Tag (VIPT) mapping. Because page offset bits (bits 11:0 in a 4 KiB page) are identical in virtual and physical address spaces, cache index lookups and TLB tag translations occur concurrently within a single clock cycle.</li>
            <li><strong>Autonomous Hardware Page Table Walker (PTW):</strong> When a DTLB or ITLB miss occurs, the hardware PTW takes ownership of the memory interface, walking the 3-level Sv39 page table in memory (Level 2 &rarr; Level 1 &rarr; Level 0) across consecutive memory reads before repopulating the TLB entry with zero software OS trap overhead.</li>
            <li><strong>Silicon Area &amp; Power Density:</strong> Synthesizes to ~135 kGE of standard cell logic (excluding cache SRAM macros). In a modern 16nm/22nm process, a complete Rocket core tile occupies less than 0.18 mm&sup2; and consumes under 25 mW at 1.0 GHz, making it the benchmark reference for energy-constrained edge Linux platforms.</li>
          </ul>

