  <!-- SECTION 3: ARCHITECTURAL SIMILARITIES & COMMON DNA         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section id="architectural-similarities" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-accent">&sect; 3. COMMON ARCHITECTURAL DNA</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">Cross-Core Similarities: The Shared RV64GC Linux Foundation</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        Despite massive divergences in pipeline depth (5-stage in-order vs. 10-stage out-of-order) and silicon gate count (135 kGE to 420 kGE), all evaluated application cores converge on a non-negotiable architectural baseline. This strict standard compliance is what enables identical, unmodified Linux kernel binaries, OpenSBI firmware, and glibc user applications to boot and execute with 100% binary portability across diverse open silicon implementations.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="similarities-grid">
      <!-- Similarity 1: Full RV64GC Conformance -->
      <div class="sim-card sim-isa">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num num-teal">01</span>
          <h4>Complete RV64GC ISA Baseline &amp; Extension Conformance</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="sim-lead">
          Every evaluated core implements the full, ratified <strong>RV64GC</strong> (<code>RV64IMAFDC_Zicsr_Zifencei</code>) instruction set architecture, guaranteeing software execution compatibility across all user-mode and kernel binaries:
        </p>
        <div class="sim-points-stack">
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">64-Bit General-Purpose Registers</span>
            <span class="point-desc">32 architectural GPRs (<code>x0&ndash;x31</code>) with <code>x0</code> permanently hardwired to zero, supporting 64-bit word operations, 64-bit pointer arithmetic, and dedicated 32-bit sub-word instructions (<code>addw</code>, <code>subw</code>, <code>sllw</code>, <code>srlw</code>, <code>sraw</code>).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Hardware Multiply / Divide (M)</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Full 64-bit integer multiplication (<code>mul</code>, <code>mulh</code>, <code>mulhsu</code>, <code>mulhu</code>) and signed/unsigned division and remainder (<code>div</code>, <code>divu</code>, <code>rem</code>, <code>remu</code>, <code>divw</code>, <code>remw</code>).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Atomic Memory Primitives (A)</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Load-Reserved / Store-Conditional (<code>lr.w/sc.w</code>, <code>lr.d/sc.d</code>) and Atomic Memory Operations (<code>amoswap</code>, <code>amoadd</code>, <code>amoxor</code>, <code>amoand</code>, <code>amoor</code>, <code>amomin</code>, <code>amomax</code>) with acquire (<code>.aq</code>) and release (<code>.rl</code>) memory ordering semantics, forming the non-negotiable foundation for Linux SMP spinlocks, mutexes, and futex synchronization.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">IEEE-754 Single &amp; Double Precision (F/D)</span>
            <span class="point-desc">32 dedicated floating-point registers (<code>f0&ndash;f31</code>), dynamic rounding modes (<code>rm</code>), status flags (<code>fflags</code>), and hardware Fused Multiply-Add (<code>fmadd</code>, <code>fmsub</code>, <code>fnmadd</code>, <code>fnmsub</code>) compliant with the standard RISC-V floating-point calling convention.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Compressed 16-Bit Encodings (C)</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Substantially improves instruction cache density and reduces bus bandwidth pressure by compressing common 32-bit instructions into 16-bit equivalents, yielding an average 25&ndash;35% code size reduction in compiled Linux kernels.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Similarity 2: Tri-Privilege Hierarchy & Delegation -->
      <div class="sim-card sim-mmode">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num num-purple">02</span>
          <h4>Tri-Privilege Hierarchy (M / S / U) &amp; Hardware Trap Delegation</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="sim-lead">
          Full operating systems require hardware-enforced protection boundaries to isolate the OS kernel from untrusted user processes and low-level firmware:
        </p>
        <div class="sim-points-stack">
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Three Distinct Privilege Rings</span>
            <span class="point-desc">User Mode (U-mode, ring 0) for unprivileged userland binaries, Supervisor Mode (S-mode, ring 1) for the Linux kernel and device drivers, and Machine Mode (M-mode, ring 3) for trusted boot firmware and OpenSBI execution.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Autonomous Trap Delegation (mideleg / medeleg)</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Machine-level delegation registers allow timer interrupts, software interrupts, page faults, and supervisor system calls (<code>ecall</code>) to bypass M-mode firmware entirely and vector directly into the Linux S-mode trap handler (<code>stvec</code>), slashing OS syscall overhead from hundreds of cycles to a direct pipeline redirect.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Dual CSR Hierarchy</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Mirrored status and control registers across modes: <code>mstatus/sstatus</code> (interrupt enables, privilege stacks), <code>mtvec/stvec</code> (trap vector base addresses), <code>mepc/sepc</code> (exception PCs), <code>mcause/scause</code> (trap etiology identifiers), and <code>mtval/stval</code> (faulting virtual addresses).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Physical Memory Protection (PMP Firewall)</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Up to 16 hardware PMP configuration and address registers (<code>pmpcfg0-3</code>, <code>pmpaddr0-15</code>) enforcing physical address isolation, preventing unauthorized DMA or compromised supervisor code from modifying secure M-mode firmware memory.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Similarity 3: Sv39 Virtual Memory Management -->
      <div class="sim-card sim-abi">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num num-yellow">03</span>
          <h4>Sv39 Radix-Tree Virtual Memory Management &amp; TLB Invalidation</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="sim-lead">
          All evaluated cores implement identical virtual address translation mechanics to support Linux demand paging, virtual memory mapping, and copy-on-write (CoW) forks:
        </p>
        <div class="sim-points-stack">
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Sv39 3-Level Radix Address Mapping</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Translates 39-bit virtual addresses (<code>VPN[2]</code>, <code>VPN[1]</code>, <code>VPN[0]</code>, <code>page_offset</code>) into 56-bit physical memory addresses across a 512 GiB virtual address space. Supports 4 KiB base pages, 2 MiB megapages, and 1 GiB gigapages.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Standard 64-Bit PTE Layout</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Ratified page table entry bitfields including Valid (<code>V</code>), Read/Write/Execute (<code>R/W/X</code>), User (<code>U</code>), Global (<code>G</code>), Accessed (<code>A</code>), Dirty (<code>D</code>), and supervisor PBMT (Physical-Memory-Attribute) metadata.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">The satp Control Register</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Standardized Address Translation and Protection register (<code>satp</code>) controlling MMU mode (<code>MODE=8</code> for Sv39), 16-bit Address Space Identifier (<code>ASID</code>), and the root page table physical address (<code>satp.PPN</code>).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">TLB Invalidation Semantics (SFENCE.VMA)</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Complete support for the <code>sfence.vma rs1, rs2</code> instruction, allowing the Linux kernel to surgically invalidate TLB translation caches per-virtual address, per-ASID, or globally across full process context switches.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Similarity 4: Unified Linux Software Ecosystem -->
      <div class="sim-card sim-bench">
        <div class="sim-header">
          <span class="sim-num num-lime">04</span>
          <h4>Unified Linux Software Ecosystem, OpenSBI &amp; Deterministic SCORE Verification</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="sim-lead">
          The shared architectural baseline ensures complete cross-platform software portability from bootloader to userland applications:
        </p>
        <div class="sim-points-stack">
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Standard GNU LP64D Calling Convention</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Cross-core binary ABI compatibility where integer parameters and return values are passed in <code>a0&ndash;a7</code>, floating-point arguments in <code>fa0&ndash;fa7</code>, stack pointer <code>sp</code> is strictly 16-byte aligned, and callee-saved registers (<code>s0&ndash;s11</code>, <code>fs0&ndash;fs11</code>) are preserved.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">OpenSBI (Supervisor Binary Interface) Parity</span>
            <span class="point-desc">All cores execute upstream OpenSBI v1.x firmware, providing standardized services for inter-processor interrupts (IPI), remote TLB shootdowns (<code>rfence</code>), timer alarms, and power management (HSM).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Upstream Linux Kernel Boot Compatibility</span>
            <span class="point-desc">A single precompiled Linux kernel image (<code>Image</code>) and Busybox/Buildroot root filesystem boots interchangeably across Rocket Chip, CVA6, BlackParrot, and BOOM without modifying a single line of C code.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="sim-point-row">
            <span class="point-badge">Cycle-Accurate SCORE Harness Verification</span>
            <span class="point-desc">Every core is evaluated under the <code>app_verilator</code> testbench profile with deterministic memory sweeps (unaligned memcpy, dirty page-table refills, PMP security violation traps) and automated UART console validation.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION 4: MICROARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENCES & DIVERSITY       -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section id="microarchitectural-differences" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-coral">&sect; 4. MICROARCHITECTURAL DIVERSITY</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">Cross-Core Differences: Engineering Trade-Offs &amp; Diversity</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        While strict RV64GC compliance guarantees software portability, the underlying microarchitectural implementations diverge dramatically. Engineering teams make radically different silicon trade-offs across issue width, memory concurrency (MSHRs), system interconnects, and speculative branch prediction to satisfy competing power, area, and throughput targets.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Differences Dimension Cards -->
    <div class="diff-cards-deck">
      <!-- Difference 1: Issue Width & Pipeline Scheduling -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-hazards">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-coral">PIPELINE SCHEDULING</span>
          <h4>Issue Width &amp; Hazard Resolution: In-Order vs. Scoreboard vs. Out-of-Order PRF</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          The instruction dispatch and dependency-tracking strategy dictates how the processor handles data hazards and long-latency multicycle operations:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">5-Stage Rigid In-Order (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Single-issue in-order dispatch. Stalls the entire front-end on multicycle operations (FPU division, unaligned memory accesses, or DRAM cache misses). Eliminates register rename logic and reservation stations, achieving the smallest gate count (~135 kGE) and lowest dynamic power.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">6-Stage Decoupled Scoreboard (CVA6 / Ariane)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decouples instruction issue from execution via a central scoreboard table. Independent 1-cycle integer ALU instructions can bypass long-latency operations (division or floating-point FMA), while results commit to architectural state strictly in program order to guarantee precise traps.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Modular Elastic Scoreboard (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decoupled Front-End (FE) and Back-End (BE) connected by credit-based elastic FIFOs. Features scoreboard-based dependency resolution with dual issue channels and distributed trap handling across modular partitions.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">3-Wide Out-of-Order with Unified PRF (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Fetches 4 instructions and renames/dispatches 3 uOPs per clock cycle. Maps 32 architectural registers onto a 128-entry Physical Register File (PRF), completely eliminating false WAR/WAW hazards. Instructions execute speculatively out-of-order via distributed reservation stations and commit through a 128-entry circular Reorder Buffer (RoB).</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 2: Memory Wall & MSHR Concurrency -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-math">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-yellow">MEMORY WALL CONCURRENCY</span>
          <h4>Cache Miss Concurrency: Blocking vs. Non-Blocking MSHR Pools</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          A major bottleneck in 64-bit application computing is the Memory Wall: servicing a DRAM line fill takes 50 to 100 clock cycles. Cores diverge fundamentally in how they handle memory latency:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Blocking Single-Miss Cache (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">4-way set-associative VIPT cache without MSHRs. On a cache miss, the pipeline freezes completely until the TileLink-UH bus refills the entire 64-byte line from DRAM. Instantaneous CPI degrades to 50&ndash;100 cycles during DRAM latency.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Non-Blocking Hit-Under-Miss (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">The L1 Data Cache incorporates dedicated Miss Status Holding Registers. Subsequent load and store instructions that hit in L1-D continue executing uninterrupted while a prior cache miss is being serviced across the BedRock interconnect.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">8-Entry Concurrent MSHR Pool (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Features an 8-entry MSHR pool supporting both "hits-under-miss" and "misses-under-miss". Sustains up to 8 concurrent outstanding 64-byte DRAM line requests simultaneously. Combined with a 16-entry Load Queue (LDQ) and 16-entry Store Queue (STQ) providing speculative load bypassing and dynamic store-to-load forwarding.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 3: System Interconnect & Coherence Fabric -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-memory">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-teal">SYSTEM INTERCONNECT</span>
          <h4>System Bus Interfaces &amp; Multi-Core Coherence Fabrics</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          Interconnect architecture dictates how easily an application core integrates into industrial SoC platforms and how effectively it scales across multi-core cluster configurations:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">TileLink-UH 64b (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decoupled open-source bus protocol with independent Channel A (requests) and Channel D (responses). Highly optimized for low-gate-count single-core SoCs, but multi-core coherence requires adding a centralized TileLink Broadcast Hub or complex L2 directory controller.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Standard Industrial AXI4-64b (CVA6 / Ariane)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Full ARM AMBA AXI4 master interface with 5 independent channels (AW, W, B, AR, R). Provides native plug-and-play compatibility with commercial ASIC memory controllers, PCIe root complexes, and standard FPGA IP blocks without bridge translation logic.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">BedRock Directory MESI &amp; 2D-Mesh NoC (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Embeds an on-chip Coherence Cache Engine (CCE) running the formal BedRock directory-based MESI protocol over credit-based packet FIFOs. Includes an integrated 5-port 2D-mesh packetized wormhole router directly inside each tile, supporting linear multi-core scaling up to 64 cores without broadcast storms.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Multi-Channel TileLink-C Coherent Bus (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Full 5-channel TileLink Coherent (TL-C) fabric with independent acquire, probe, and grant channels, interfacing with an interleaved multi-bank shared L2 cache and hardware directory snooper for high-bandwidth superscalar memory pipelines.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Difference 4: Branch Prediction & Speculative Window -->
      <div class="diff-spec-card diff-security">
        <div class="diff-spec-head">
          <span class="diff-tag badge-purple">BRANCH SPECULATION</span>
          <h4>Dynamic Branch Predictors &amp; Speculative Recovery Windows</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="diff-lead">
          As pipeline depth increases, the penalty for branch mispredictions escalates from 2 cycles to over 10 cycles, driving increasingly sophisticated branch prediction hardware:
        </p>
        <div class="diff-points-stack">
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">28-Entry BTB + 2-Bit BHT (Rocket Chip)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Simple Branch Target Buffer (28 entries) with a 2-bit saturating counter array. Branches resolve in the EX stage with a modest 2-cycle bubble penalty on mispredicts. Minimal silicon area footprint (~2.5 kGE).</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Two-Level BHT + BTB + 4-Entry RAS (CVA6 / Ariane)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Combines a 2-level Branch History Table (BHT), Branch Target Buffer, and 4-entry Return Address Stack (RAS). Bounding mispredict penalties strictly to 3 clock cycles.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Modular Elastic BPU (BlackParrot)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Decoupled branch prediction engine located in the Front-End partition, feeding an elastic instruction queue to absorb redirect latency from the execution back-end.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="diff-point-row">
            <span class="diff-point-title">Advanced TAGE Predictor + 32-Entry RAS + FTQ (SonicBOOM)</span>
            <span class="diff-point-text">Deploys an industry-grade TAgged GEometric history length (TAGE) branch predictor with multi-component global history tables, Next-Line Predictor (NLP), 32-entry RAS, and a 16-entry Fetch Target Queue (FTQ). Achieves &gt;96% accuracy on complex branch-heavy Linux workloads, avoiding devastating 8-to-10 cycle pipeline flushes.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Side-by-Side Architectural Differences Matrix Table -->
    <div class="table-title-box">
      <div class="table-title-flex">
        <div>
          <h3 class="table-heading">Side-by-Side Architectural Differences Matrix</h3>
          <p class="table-caption">Comprehensive structural comparison across evaluated open-source 64-bit application cores.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="matrix-filter-bar">
          <button class="matrix-filter-btn active" data-tier="all">All Cores</button>
