  <!-- SECTION 6: CONFIGURATION SENSITIVITY DEEP-DIVE -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <section id="configuration-scaling" class="content-block">
    <div class="block-header-wrap">
      <div class="badge badge-accent">&sect; 6. CONFIGURATION SENSITIVITY &amp; PARAMETRIC SCALING</div>
      <h2 class="block-title">Multi-Configuration Scaling: Microarchitectural Tuning Impact</h2>
      <p class="block-lead">
        A 64-bit application core is not a static point in design space—it is an extensible silicon generator. Parametric switches—such as expanding superscalar issue width from 1-wide to 4-wide, doubling L1 cache depth, adding non-blocking MSHR registers, or scaling from a single core to an 8-tile coherent mesh—dramatically reshape performance, clock frequency, and silicon gate count.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Pareto Frontier Summary Banner (Top-Down Progressive Flow) -->
    <div class="pareto-summary-topdown">
      <!-- Dimension 1: BOOM -->
      <div class="pareto-step-card border-yellow">
        <div class="pareto-step-header">
          <div class="pareto-badge badge-yellow">DIMENSION 1: SUPERSCALAR ISSUE WIDTH &amp; WINDOW SCALING</div>
          <div class="pareto-kpi-chip">
            <span class="pareto-stat text-accent">+41.5% Bandwidth</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pareto-step-body">
          <div class="pareto-title-row">
            <h4 class="pareto-step-title">BOOM Superscalar Scaling: SmallBOOM &rarr; MegaBOOM</h4>
            <span class="pareto-sub">1-Wide (595.0 MB/s) &rarr; 4-Wide (842.0 MB/s) &bull; 245 kGE &rarr; 580 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p class="pareto-desc">
            Scaling out-of-order decode/issue width from 1-wide to 4-wide and quadrupling Reorder Buffer depth (16 &rarr; 128 RoB entries) hides high-latency DRAM misses, delivering +41.5% memory bandwidth gain. However, wire delays in the multi-ported 160-entry Physical Register File (PRF) introduce diminishing PPA returns above 3-wide issue.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 1 -> 2 -->
      <div class="pareto-connector">
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
        <div class="connector-pill">
          <span>&darr; Dimension Shift: Cache Capacity Scaling vs Arithmetic Stalls (+49.7% Memory Gain)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Dimension 2: Rocket -->
      <div class="pareto-step-card border-blue">
        <div class="pareto-step-header">
          <div class="pareto-badge badge-blue">DIMENSION 2: L1 CACHE CAPACITY &amp; LINEAR SWEEP EXPANSION</div>
          <div class="pareto-kpi-chip">
            <span class="pareto-stat text-teal">+49.7% Bandwidth</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pareto-step-body">
          <div class="pareto-title-row">
            <h4 class="pareto-step-title">Rocket Cache Expansion: 4 KiB &rarr; 32 KiB</h4>
            <span class="pareto-sub">4 KiB (342.0 MB/s) &rarr; 32 KiB (512.0 MB/s) &bull; 98 kGE &rarr; 151 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p class="pareto-desc">
            In lean 5-stage in-order pipelines, linear streaming memory operations suffer catastrophic cache thrashing when working sets exceed L1 size. Expanding L1 cache from 4 KiB to 32 KiB eliminates miss-under-miss stall bubbles, delivering a massive +49.7% throughput leap at a modest +53 kGE silicon cost.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 2 -> 3 -->
      <div class="pareto-connector">
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
        <div class="connector-pill">
          <span>&darr; Dimension Shift: Out-of-Order Scoreboard Depth vs Pipelining (+8.1% Bandwidth)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Dimension 3: CVA6 -->
      <div class="pareto-step-card border-teal">
        <div class="pareto-step-header">
          <div class="pareto-badge badge-teal">DIMENSION 3: SCOREBOARD INSTRUCTION WINDOW DEPTH</div>
          <div class="pareto-kpi-chip">
            <span class="pareto-stat text-lime">+8.1% Bandwidth</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pareto-step-body">
          <div class="pareto-title-row">
            <h4 class="pareto-step-title">CVA6 Scoreboard Depth: 8-Entry &rarr; 16-Entry</h4>
            <span class="pareto-sub">8-Entry (442.8 MB/s) &rarr; 16-Entry (478.5 MB/s) &bull; 135 kGE &rarr; 158 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p class="pareto-desc">
            Expanding the instruction scoreboard from 8 to 16 entries enables non-blocking tracking of in-flight register dependencies. Independent memory operations bypass long multicycle operations (integer division, floating-point FMAs) without stalling the issue stage, yielding an +8.1% throughput boost.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 3 -> 4 -->
      <div class="pareto-connector">
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
        <div class="connector-pill">
          <span>&darr; Dimension Shift: Scale-Out Multicore Coherence vs Bus Contention (88% Efficiency Across 8 Cores)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="connector-line"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Dimension 4: BlackParrot -->
      <div class="pareto-step-card border-coral">
        <div class="pareto-step-header">
          <div class="pareto-badge badge-coral">DIMENSION 4: COHERENT MULTICORE CLUSTERING &amp; NOC SCALING</div>
          <div class="pareto-kpi-chip">
            <span class="pareto-stat text-coral">88% Per-Core Eff.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pareto-step-body">
          <div class="pareto-title-row">
            <h4 class="pareto-step-title">BlackParrot SMP Multicore: 1 Core &rarr; 8 Cores</h4>
            <span class="pareto-sub">1 Core (425.0 MB/s) &rarr; 8 Cores (3,001.0 MB/s Agg.) &bull; 165 kGE &rarr; 1,320 kGE</span>
          </div>
          <p class="pareto-desc">
            Scaling from a single core tile to an 8-tile cluster connected over a 2D-mesh wormhole NoC maintains 88% per-core memory efficiency. The formally verified BedRock directory-based MESI cache coherence engine prevents bus crossbar saturation and enables seamless horizontal scaling.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sensitivity-container">
      {appCoreConfigSensitivity.map((fam) => (
        <div class="family-scaling-card">
          <div class="fam-head-flex">
            <div>
              <div class="fam-sub-wrap">
                <span class={`badge ${fam.badgeClass}`}>{fam.badge}</span>
              </div>
              <h3 class="fam-title">{fam.family}</h3>
              <p class="fam-desc">{fam.description}</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          {fam.family.startsWith("SonicBOOM") && (
            <div class="boom-scaling-ladder">
              <div class="ladder-sub-banner">
                <span class="badge badge-yellow">PROGRESSIVE LADDER</span>
                <span class="ladder-banner-txt">SonicBOOM Issue Width &amp; Execution Window Scaling (1-Wide &rarr; 4-Wide)</span>
              </div>

              <!-- Step 1: SmallBOOM -->
              <div class="boom-step-card">
                <div class="boom-step-head">
                  <span class="boom-step-badge badge-yellow">STEP 1 &bull; 1-WIDE</span>
                  <div class="boom-step-title-wrap">
                    <h4 class="boom-step-title">SmallBOOM (1-Wide In-Order Fetch / OoO Commit)</h4>
                    <span class="boom-step-sub">1-Wide Decode &bull; 64 PRF &bull; 16 RoB &bull; 16K/16K L1 (4 MSHRs)</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="boom-step-metrics">
                    <span class="score-badge">595.0 MB/s</span>
                    <span class="meta-pill">1.3 GHz &bull; 245 kGE</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p class="boom-step-desc">
                  Minimalist out-of-order engine. Achieves 76% of LargeBOOM memory throughput at only 58% silicon area. Ideal for power-constrained Linux smart edge cameras.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- Connector 1 -> 2 -->
              <div class="boom-connector">
                <div class="connector-line"></div>
                <div class="connector-pill">
                  <span>&darr; +95.4 MB/s (+16.0% Throughput) &bull; Dual-Issue Decode &amp; 96 PRF (+85 kGE)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="connector-line"></div>
              </div>

              <!-- Step 2: MediumBOOM -->
              <div class="boom-step-card">
                <div class="boom-step-head">
                  <span class="boom-step-badge badge-cyan">STEP 2 &bull; 2-WIDE</span>
                  <div class="boom-step-title-wrap">
                    <h4 class="boom-step-title">MediumBOOM (2-Wide Balanced Superscalar)</h4>
                    <span class="boom-step-sub">2-Wide Decode &bull; 96 PRF &bull; 64 RoB &bull; 32K/32K L1 (6 MSHRs)</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="boom-step-metrics">
                    <span class="score-badge">690.4 MB/s</span>
                    <span class="meta-pill">1.4 GHz &bull; 330 kGE</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p class="boom-step-desc">
                  Balanced PPA sweet-spot. Dual-issue superscalar pipeline eliminates front-end instruction starvation, delivering 88% peak bandwidth with modest 330 kGE footprint.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- Connector 2 -> 3 -->
              <div class="boom-connector">
                <div class="connector-line"></div>
                <div class="connector-pill">
                  <span>&darr; +92.1 MB/s (+13.3% Throughput) &bull; 3-Wide Dispatch &amp; 8 MSHRs (+90 kGE Flagship)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="connector-line"></div>
              </div>

              <!-- Step 3: LargeBOOM -->
              <div class="boom-step-card">
                <div class="boom-step-head">
                  <span class="boom-step-badge badge-coral">STEP 3 &bull; 3-WIDE</span>
                  <div class="boom-step-title-wrap">
                    <h4 class="boom-step-title">LargeBOOM (3-Wide Berkeley Flagship)</h4>
                    <span class="boom-step-sub">3-Wide Decode &bull; 128 PRF &bull; 96 RoB &bull; 32K/32K L1 (8 MSHRs)</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="boom-step-metrics">
                    <span class="score-badge">782.5 MB/s</span>
                    <span class="meta-pill">1.5 GHz &bull; 420 kGE</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <p class="boom-step-desc">
                  SCORE benchmark baseline champion. Deep 8-entry MSHR pool fully overlaps L1 misses with ALU compute. Flagship configuration in Berkeley RISC-V testchips.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- Connector 3 -> 4 -->
              <div class="boom-connector">
                <div class="connector-line"></div>
                <div class="connector-pill">
                  <span>&darr; +59.5 MB/s (+7.6% Throughput) &bull; 4-Wide Extreme Commit &amp; 160 PRF (+160 kGE &bull; Diminishing PPA Returns)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="connector-line"></div>
              </div>

              <!-- Step 4: MegaBOOM -->
              <div class="boom-step-card">
                <div class="boom-step-head">
